---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import PageShell from '../components/site/PageShell.astro'
---

<Base title="Notes · R3F v10 Astro Starter">
  <PageShell
    eyebrow="notes"
    title="Alpha rough edges"
    lede="Everything here cost us time at least once. Both alphas move fast, so check the dates on anything you read elsewhere."
  >
    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>You must pass renderer to &lt;Canvas&gt;</h2>
      <p>
        The bare flag or a config object — either gets you the new <code>WebGPURenderer</code>, which
        the TSL node materials in this scene require. Without it the alpha quietly builds the legacy
        <code>WebGLRenderer</code> and node materials take it down with a cryptic
        <code>Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'replace')</code> from inside shader
        compilation. You still have to start on the new renderer even where WebGPU is missing: the
        WebGL2 fallback is automatic and TSL handles the switch, but only if
        <code>renderer</code> put you on that path in the first place. Pass
        <code>gl={'{{ antialias: true }}'}</code> instead when you want the legacy renderer on
        purpose.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>The TSL hooks live on a separate entry</h2>
      <p>
        <code>useUniforms</code> and <code>useLocalNodes</code> come from
        <code>@react-three/fiber/webgpu</code>, not the main entry. The two share a React context
        through <code>globalThis</code>, so the hooks work inside the regular
        <code>&lt;Canvas&gt;</code> next to drei.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>useLocalNodes has the creator in its dep array</h2>
      <p>
        It is a <code>useMemo</code> keyed on the store's uniforms, nodes and textures plus the HMR
        version — which is the point, since the node graph then rebuilds when you edit the shader.
        But an inline arrow is a new identity every render, so wrap the creator in
        <code>useCallback</code> or the graph rebuilds on every render.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>Uniforms read back out of the store need a cast</h2>
      <p>
        They come back typed <code>UniformNode&lt;unknown&gt;</code>, which three's TSL operators
        reject. Launder it in one place rather than at every call site.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>useFrame state has no clock</h2>
      <p>
        The scheduler moved out into <code>@pmndrs/scheduler</code> and timing sits directly on the
        state: <code>state.elapsed</code>, <code>state.delta</code>, <code>state.frame</code>,
        <code>state.time</code>. v10 also renames <code>state.gl</code> to
        <code>state.renderer</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>drei v11 alpha ships a trimmed export set</h2>
      <p>
        <code>CameraControls</code>, <code>Grid</code> and <code>ContactShadows</code> have not landed
        yet — this starter uses <code>OrbitControls</code> (which has <code>autoRotate</code> built
        in) and three's <code>gridHelper</code> instead.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>Leva loses its styles across a view transition</h2>
      <p>
        Astro-specific, and it looks like the panel exploded. Leva styles itself with stitches, which
        injects rules straight into the CSSOM via <code>insertRule</code> — the
        <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> tags it creates have no text content. The router swaps
        <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> wholesale on navigation, so those nodes are dropped and the
        persisted panel comes back with no CSS at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fix is in <code>Base.astro</code>: on <code>astro:before-swap</code>, tag every
        runtime-injected style with a <code>data-astro-transition-persist</code> id and add a
        matching placeholder to the incoming document. That is what makes Astro keep the live node —
        rules and all — instead of replacing it. Anything else that injects styles at runtime needs
        the same treatment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>A zero-height parent boots nothing</h2>
      <p>
        Astro-specific in practice: drop the island straight into <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> and R3F's
        100%-of-nothing container collapses. You get a blank page and no error. Give the wrapper a
        real size — here <code>fixed inset-0</code>, inside the component so it moves with the island.
      </p>
      <p>Background on the port is in <a href="/about">/about</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </PageShell>
</Base>
